    <page>
   <!-- Need to revise the following Learning Target 7 (Attempt 3) -->

        <exercise workspace="1in">
            <!-- Learning Target 7 (Attempt 3) -->
            <title>Learning Target 7, Last Attempt</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>
                    I can find the matrix of a linear transformation using
                    elementary vectors, apply a transformation to a vector or
                    shape, and compose transformations using matrix
                    multiplication.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Let <m>T:\mathbb{R}^{2}\to\mathbb{R}^{2}</m> that
                        reflects vectors across the <m>y</m>-axis.
                        Find the matrix <m>A</m> such that
                        <m>T({\xvec}) = A{\xvec}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find <m>T\left( \twovec{3}{4}\right)</m> using your
                        answer from part (a).
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Let <m>S:\mathbb{R}^{2} \to \mathbb{R}^{2}</m> be
                        defined by <m>S({\xvec}) = B{\xvec}</m>.
                        Is the matrix representing <m>S\circ T</m> equal to
                        <m>AB</m> or <m>BA</m>?
                        Explain.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <exercise workspace="1in">
            <!-- Learning Target 6 (Attempt 2) -->
            <title>Learning Target 6, Last Attempt</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>
                    I can reformulate and answer questions about linearly
                    dependent or linearly independent vectors using appropriate
                    computations, and explain the meaning of the results of
                    computations in the language of linear combinations.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <alert><em>Note</em></alert>: In this problem, if you make a
                    small entry error in Sage, you will likely radically change
                    the conclusions you make.
                    <em>Be certain to double check what you enter in Sage to make 
                        sure it matches what you want it to represent.</em>
                </p>

               
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
               <p>
                                  
                                  <m>\{\vvec_{1}, \vvec_{2}, \vvec_{3}\}</m> where
                                  <m>\vvec_{1} = \begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 2 \\ 3\end{bmatrix}</m>,
                                  <m>\vvec_{2} = \begin{bmatrix}4 \\ 5 \\ 6\end{bmatrix}</m> and
                                  <m>\vvec_{3} = \begin{bmatrix}2 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{bmatrix}</m> linearly
                                  dependent or independent?
                                  Why?
                              </p>

                            </statement>
                        </task>
                <task>
                <statement>
                              <p>
                                  
                                  Is the set of vectors
                                  <m>\{\wvec_{1}, \wvec_{2}, \wvec_{3}\}</m> where
                                  <m>\wvec_{1} = \begin{bmatrix}0 \\ 1 \\ 5\end{bmatrix}</m>,
                                  <m>\wvec_{2} = \begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 2 \\ 8\end{bmatrix}</m>, and
                                  <m>\wvec_{3} = \begin{bmatrix}4 \\ -1 \\ 0\end{bmatrix}</m> linearly
                                  dependent or linearly independent?
                                  Why?
                              </p>
               
                                 </statement>
                        </task>
                        <task>
                            <statement>

        
                              <p>
                                  The set
                                  <m>\{\vvec_{1}, \vvec_{2}, \vvec_{3}, \wvec_{1}\}</m>
                                  (using the ones given in #1 and #2) is linearly dependent.
                                  Show this by writing one of the vectors as a linear combination of the other
                                  vectors.
                              </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    
